**First-Person Account: An Egyptian Musician’s Reflection on Cymbals and the Dulcimer**

I am a musician in the service of the temple, and my hands know the weight of two sacred instruments: the cymbals and the dulcimer. Today, after a long day of performance at a festival honoring the gods, I sit in the shade of a palm and write down my thoughts, for I feel the need to record what these instruments mean to our people.

The cymbals are my constant companions. They are made of bronze, shaped much like those I have seen in the tombs of our ancestors—two pairs, each a perfect circle of sound. I hold them in my palms, one in each hand, and when I strike them together, a bright, clashing tone rings out—a sound that commands attention and lifts the spirit. They are especially dear to the sacred musicians;
